course: everyday
lesson: 425
title: Family game night
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The whole of Mia's family played a racing game on Saturday, and everyone was sure Grandad would come last: he held the controller upside down for the first lap. Then he won. Three times. It turned out he had driven a delivery van for thirty years and «knew how corners work». Mia's dad demanded a rematch, lost again, and went to make tea «for no reason». Before they started, Mia's mum had collected everyone's phones in the fruit bowl, and nobody missed them once.
explain: Theo's mum has been the family champion at a board game for six years. Theo has lost about two hundred times. Last Friday he finally won, jumped onto the sofa, and then saw his mum's face, and for one second worried she was upset. She was grinning: «Six years I've been waiting for someone to beat me properly.» She hadn't let him win, and that is exactly why the win felt so good. Noah's dad plays a quiz game as if he knows everything. He told everyone with great confidence that a spider has six legs. The family has not let him forget it.
explain: In Ruby's house it is the dance game. Ruby taught her dad the moves, and he danced like a wardrobe falling down the stairs, but he kept going, laughing louder every round, and Ruby laughed so much she could not dance either. Jayden's grandma brought an old card game from when she was a girl, and beat the whole family at it, including Jayden's uncle, who «doesn't lose at cards». Zara's family plays Minecraft together on one big screen, and her little brother mainly builds toilets.
explain: Some families pick games where nobody loses alone. Ollie's family plays cooperative games, where everyone works together against the game, and wins or loses together, so nobody sulks. Leo's family plays on mixed teams, grown-ups and kids together, so the kids are not always beaten by the grown-ups, and the grown-ups have to explain things nicely. Kai's family plays a party game every Friday, and the best part, Kai says, is not the game: it is the hour when nobody is in a hurry.
fact: Family game nights are a way to spend time together and laugh, not only to win.
fact: Older people can surprise you, because they bring skills and experience from their whole lives.
fact: A win feels real and earned when the other player did not let you win.
fact: A good winner is happy without rubbing it in, and a good loser congratulates the winner.
fact: In cooperative games the players work together against the game and win or lose as a team.
fact: Mixed teams of grown-ups and children make games fairer and make people explain things kindly.
fact: Putting phones away during game night helps everyone pay attention to each other.
fact: Being bad at a game and laughing about it can be the most fun part of the evening.
fact: A spider has eight legs, and an insect has six.
fact: Old card and board games from grandparents' childhoods are still fun to play today.
exercise: Why did everyone expect Grandad to come last? | He held the controller upside down at first | He said he had always hated racing games of every kind | He had never sat in a car | He had fallen asleep on the sofa
exercise: Why did Grandad really win the races? | He had years of driving experience | The game let old people win | Mia secretly helped him steer round the corners | Everyone else let him win
exercise: How did Mia's dad probably feel after losing again? | A bit embarrassed and keen to hide it | Proud that he was the best player | Sad that the game was far too easy for grown-ups | Bored and ready to go to bed
exercise: What did Mia's mum do with the phones? | Collected them in the fruit bowl | Gave them to Grandad to look after | Let everyone keep them on silent mode | Hid them in the washing machine for the night
exercise: Why did Theo's win feel so good? | His mum had not let him win | His mum was not trying at all | He had cheated just a little bit | He had read the rules in secret
exercise: What did Theo worry about after winning? | That his mum might be upset | That he had broken the game somehow | That his win would not count | That he had to tidy up the game
exercise: What made Theo's mum a good loser? | She was pleased and praised his win | She said the game was unfair | She asked for a different game at once, to win | She went to bed without speaking
exercise: What mistake did Noah's dad make in the quiz? | He said a spider has six legs | He said a spider has eight legs | He said a spider has ten legs | He said a spider has no legs
exercise: Why did Ruby and her dad laugh so much at the dance game? | Dad danced badly but kept going happily | Dad was a better dancer than Ruby | The game played a very funny song | Ruby fell over at the very start of the song
exercise: What surprised Jayden's family on game night? | Grandma beat everyone at an old card game | Grandma did not know any games | The uncle won every single round | The card game had no rules at all, so anyone won
exercise: What does Zara's little brother mostly build in Minecraft? | Toilets | Lighthouses | Castles | Bridges
exercise: Why do cooperative games stop people sulking in Ollie's family? | Everyone wins or loses together | Nobody is allowed to talk during them | The grown-ups always let him win | The games have no ending at all
exercise: Why are mixed teams fairer in Leo's family? | Kids are not always beaten by grown-ups | Grown-ups are not allowed to play well or try hard | Each child gets two turns each time | The kids always choose the game
exercise: What does Kai like best about Friday party games? | The hour when nobody is in a hurry | The prizes that the winner gets | Being allowed to stay up very late | The snacks his dad makes each time
exercise: Which kind of courage did Ruby's dad show? | Being happy to look silly for fun | Refusing to dance in front of Ruby | Pretending he was a great dancer | Quitting before anybody could laugh
exercise: Which family's game night idea fits best when one child always loses and sulks? | Cooperative games where all win or lose together | Games where the oldest player always starts | Games played with phones on the table | Games played only by the grown-ups
test: Dad, smug: «spiders, 6 legs, obviously». Noah's sister: «...» What's the right correction? | No, spiders have 8; insects have 6 | Yes, all small animals have 6 legs | No, spiders have 10 legs | Yes, but only big spiders have 6
test: Grandad holding the controller upside down. Mia's cousin: «easy win lol». What happened? | Grandad won three times in a row | Grandad gave up after just one lap | Grandad crashed every single race | Grandad just watched and did not play
test: Theo finally beats Mum after six years. Mum: «...» Which reply shows she's a good loser? | Well played, you beat me properly! | You only won because I was so tired tonight | That doesn't count, let's play again | I let you win, so don't get excited
test: Theo jumps on the sofa yelling «I WON I WON». What should he do next to be a good winner? | Thank Mum for the game and stop gloating | Keep yelling until Mum admits it | Tell his friends Mum is rubbish | Refuse to ever play again to stay champion
test: Ollie's little cousin always cries when she loses. Which game would suit tonight? | A cooperative game where all play together | A game where the loser has to do all the dishes | A quiz where the youngest goes last | A race where the grown-ups play hard
test: Mum drops everyone's phones in the fruit bowl. Dad: «what if work calls?!» Why does Mum do it? | So everyone pays attention to each other | Because phones get broken during board games | Because the fruit bowl charges them | So Dad cannot look up the answers
test: Ruby's dad dances like «a wardrobe falling down the stairs». Was the night a failure? | No, laughing together was the best part | Yes, because he lost every round | Yes, he should have refused to play | No, because he secretly won in the end
test: Jayden's uncle: «I don't lose at cards». Grandma: beats him five times. What's the lesson? | Experience can beat confidence | Uncles are bad at every game | Old games are easier to win | Grandma must have cheated
test: Leo's family on mixed teams: Dad + Leo vs Mum + Leo's sister. Why is that good? | Kids aren't always beaten and grown-ups explain | Grown-ups get to win every single time | The kids can rest while grown-ups play | Nobody needs to know the rules
test: Kai's friend: «family game night is so lame». Kai: «...» What would Kai say? | The best bit is an hour where nobody's rushing | It's lame, I only go for the snacks | It's only fun when I win every game | My parents make me go, I hate it
test: Which is a sign that a game night went well? | Everyone laughed and wants to play again | Only one person had fun all evening | Someone won and the rest went to bed upset | The game finished in five minutes flat
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a victory obtained against an opponent who did not concede deliberately is experienced as more satisfying. | The victory is genuinely earned by the player's own skill | The victory carries a monetary reward | The victory may be recorded in an official register | The victory obliges the opponent to cease playing
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a cooperative game? | The players act jointly against the game and share the outcome | The players compete individually and the eldest prevails | The players are divided by age into opposing teams | The players are prohibited from communicating
exam: It is given that a participant in a quiz asserts that a spider possesses six legs. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The assertion is incorrect, a spider possessing eight legs | The assertion is correct for all species of spider | The assertion is incorrect, a spider possessing ten legs | The assertion is correct for spiders of large size
exam: Determine which conduct most accurately exemplifies that of a gracious loser. | Congratulating the winner upon the victory obtained | Disputing the validity of the result obtained | Declining to participate in any further game | Asserting that the victory was conceded deliberately
